  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Typical summer weather today and Monday with \nnear average temperatures and late afternoon-evening showers/storms. \n\nNorthwest upper/mid level flow has set up over the forecast \narea now that the axis of a seasonably deep trough has shifted \noffshore. At the surface, a cold front is positioned southeast \nof the NC coast and high pressure has moved in over the Mid-\nAtlantic. Today actually looks pretty decent overall with highs \nin the mid 80s and partly to mostly sunny skies. Could be a \ntouch humid, but it's definitely not the worst humidity we've \nseen. Later this evening, a shortwave traveling down the trough \nis expected to trigger at least a few thunderstorms across the \nMid-Atlantic. CAMs indicate a relatively late initiation \n(~7-9pm) and storms staying mostly in the Piedmont. The later \nstart time would of course mean waning instability, but NW flow \nhas historically been good at producing storms with strong wind\ngusts around here. The 25-30kt of shear expected is pretty good\nfor July. While storms will be isolated to widely scattered, \nthere is the potential for one or two stronger storms that \nproduce damaging wind gusts or even some hail. SPC has put the \npiedmont in a Marginal Risk for today. Another round of \nscattered storms is expected Monday, but with an earlier \ninitiation time and coastal areas being included in the action.\nWill still have that NW flow in place, about 30kt of bulk \nshear, and 1500-2000 J/kg of CAPE, so stronger storms will still\nbe possible. SPC also has a marginal risk for much of the area \nMonday. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Widespread showers and thunderstorms expected \nTuesday afternoon with the potential for damaging wind gusts and \nhail. \n\nTuesday still looks like the best shot at widespread thunderstorms \nand at least scattered instances of severe storms. The Mid-Atlantic \nwill still be under NW flow as a cold front sweeps through at the \nsurface. The GFS and Canadian have trended toward the more aggressive \nECMWF solution regarding the upper trough. Now all three want to cut \nthe low off somewhere over the upper Mid-Atlantic or southern New \nEngland, but there are differences in timing. Regardless, confidence \nis increasing in a strong synoptic set up for severe weather. Bulk \nshear of 35kt and MLCAPE of 1000-1500 J/kg would be sufficient \nsupport. Main threats would be damaging wind gusts and large hail. \nAs always though, timing of the storms will be important to how much \nsevere WX occurs (as has been the case with some recent events). \nMorning showers may inhibit instability, which could also lead \nto a bust. It is likely that the SPC will carry over the slight \nrisk from the day 4 extended outlook into the day 3 outlook, \nwhich will come out later this morning.",
